ack, speed of approach, or frontal position, or say how fast the feature may reach here.\n\nAt basin scale, the call is a weak local gradient with lower pressure to the north, while the broader ring is moderate. The call's local 24-hour pressure tendency is +2.1 hPa, while the broader three-day ring shows pressure overhead falling; the time scales differ. Across the five Nyanza Basin points, daily mean pressure trends lower from Wednesday to Friday; the southern point remains highest and the pattern is otherwise broadly uniform. That broad fall supports increasing convergence and scattered convective rain risk, especially where the local rain signal is strongest, but it does not imply a single basin-wide downpour. The Kisumu-area signal is lighter than the strongest basin point, consistent with uneven showers and thunderstorms.\n\nObserved so far today: As of 06:01, reports through 05:00: no rain; no thunder; high so far 20°C / 68°F; low so far 19°C / 66°F; peak sustained 9 km/h; sky 4/8.\n\nThe fresh ground air-quality readings were Kisumu Airport 66, Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central 50, and Dunga Beach 38. All three were three hours old and not stale. The recorded range is 38-66, with Kisumu Airport highest; the model assessment is 62, so there is no notable disparity and the overall category is Moderate.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\n\nRecent verification was given extra weight over the longer record where the two pulled differently. Although most Day+0 rain calls were correct yesterday, best_match missed; the established Day+0 ranking still prevents treating every rain call equally: GFS remains a cautious rain input, while ICON's established strength supports the rain signal. GFS missed yesterday's thunder call, whereas ECMWF, ICON, UKMO, and best match verified it; that recent thunder split keeps GFS from dominating the thunder wording. ECMWF, ICON, and UKMO systematically under-forecast peak wind in the longer record, while best_match has a slight tendency in the same direction, supporting the corrected land gust of 41.4 km/h (22 kt) rather than treating a quiet interval as safe.\n\nThe review says best_match slightly under-forecasts peak wind, but its uncorrected Day+0 gust is the largest numerical-model value in today's set. I trusted the standing bias and retained the corrected 41.4 km/h (22 kt) land gust rather than adopting that uncorrected value.\n\nKenya Meteorological Department (KMD) is a peer in today's forecast: it calls 28.0°C / 82.4°F for the high and 20.0°C / 68.0°F for the low, with sunny intervals this morning, light showers tonight, and moderate afternoon showers and thunderstorms over a few places. It agrees with the other forecasts on the afternoon thunderstorm threat, while its high is cooler than the 29.0°C / 84.2°F call. KMD's national outlook also highlights enhanced rainfall over Kisumu.\n\nAt Day+3, the review finds no meaningful model separation; at Day+7, it likewise finds no meaningful separation among the models that forecast that far. Yesterday's Day+3 rain checks were correct across the scored models, while Day+7 was mixed, so the extended outlook remains hedged. The stored record covers 43 forecast days: Day+0 is a settled picture overall, and Day+7 is settled for the models that forecast that lead; Day+3 is sufficient for comparison, but differences smaller than about 15 points remain noise. Kenya Met has fewer Day+0 checks and does not forecast beyond Day+0. Only GFS, ECMWF, and best match forecast Day+7; ICON, UKMO, and Kenya Met do not. best_match's rain probability is identical to ECMWF's in the stored record, so it was weighed once rather than as a second independent probability.\n\nThe models were last all on the same cycle at 18:00Z, 9 hours ago, so the guidance behind this is at least 9 hours old; faster runs may be newer, which widens uncertainty in storm timing and placement. The airport observation is a single point and is used only as a report of what happened there, not 